viewer *later* asks about the voice quality, directly addressing the potential issue of the low tone (B). If the interviewer was primarily concerned about the noise, they might have commented on the noise itself. Since they specifically inquire about the voice quality immediately after the repetitions, B is a more directly supported reason for potential difficulty in hearing the specific utterance.\n\n*Self-Correction/Refinement:* The question asks why the interviewer asked the respondent to repeat *multiple times*. The interviewer didn't ask the respondent to repeat; the interviewer *repeated the answer* ("Alcohol," "Alcohol") as if confirming what they heard or prompting the respondent to confirm they heard correctly over the noise/low voice. If we interpret "repeat multiple times" as the interviewer repeating the answer back, this is usually done when the interviewer is unsure what was said. Both noise (A) and low voice (B) cause uncertainty. Since the interviewer explicitly asks about the low voice later, B is a strong candidate.\n\nLet's
