les).\n\nTime for the stone to fall:\n$$H = \frac{1}{2} g t_{drop}^2 \implies t_{drop} = \sqrt{\frac{2H}{g}}$$\n\nTime for the sound to return:\n$$H = v_s t_{sound} \implies t_{sound} = \frac{H}{v_s}$$\n\nTotal time:\n$$t_{total} = t_{drop} + t_{sound} = \sqrt{\frac{2H}{g}} + \frac{H}{v_s}$$\n\n**Step 3: Solve for H using the estimated $t_{total} \approx 2.6$ s.**\n\nSince $v_s$ is very large (343 m/s), $t_{sound}$ will be much smaller than $t_{drop}$ for typical well depths. We can initially approximate $t_{total} \approx t_{drop}$.\n\nApproximation 1: $t_{total} \approx t_{drop} = 2.6 \text{ s}$\n$$H \approx \frac{1}{2} g t_{drop}^2 = \frac{1}{2} (9.8 \text{ m/s}^2) (2.6 \text{ s})^2$$\n$$H \approx 4.9 \times 6.76 \approx 33.12 \text{ meters}$$\n\n**Step 4: Refine the calculation using both terms (for $H \approx 33 \text{ m}$).**\nIf $H = 33.12 \text{ m}$:\n$$t_{drop} = \sqrt{\frac{2 \times 33.12}{9.8}} \approx \sqrt{6.76} = 2.60 \text{ s}$$\n$$t_{sound} = \frac{33.12}{343} \approx 0.096 \text{ s}$$\n$$t_{total} = 2.60 + 0.096 = 2.696 \text{ s}$$\n\nThis calculated total time (2.70 s) is very close to the estimated time from the audio (2.6 s). The slight discrepancy might be due to the imprecision of estimating the start time of the speech or the exact moment of the splash, but $H \approx 33$ meters is a robust estimate based on the observed time delay of approximately 2.6 seconds.\n\n**Step 5: Compare the result with the choices.**\nThe estimated depth is approximately
