n the woman asks why David is named Sarah, it implies the man was trying to deceive her about the contact name. If he knew the contact was named Sarah and insisted it was David, he was lying about the information presented on the phone screen.\n\nLet's re-evaluate based on the strongest evidence of deception:\nWoman: "There's a girl named Sarah calling you..."\nMan: "No, that's David, trust me." (He denies the name Sarah)\nWoman answers, confirms it is David.\nWoman: "Why is David named Sarah on your phone?"\n\nThe man lied by denying the name "Sarah" was present, even though the woman confirmed the person was David. He tried to convince her the name was David when it was clearly labeled Sarah. Therefore, he was lying about the information presented to her.\n\nThe most accurate answer is that he was lying (or at least intentionally misleading) about the contact name displayed.\n\nAnswer: $\boxed{B}$
