2025-12-22 19:07:27 | INFO | Q715 id=BV1Yy4y1G7fG_00-00-02_00-00-12 | pred=B | gold=B | correct=True | running_acc=65.03% | raw=The audio contains the distinct sound of an aircraft, specifically a propeller plane, which is characterized by a rhythmic, cyclical sound. The sound intensity and frequency characteristics of the engine noise and propeller wash change over time.\n\nStep 1: Analyze the sound characteristics. The sound starts at a moderate volume and then rapidly increases in volume and pitch (Doppler effect), indicating the aircraft is approaching.\nStep 2: Observe the change in sound over the duration of the clip. The sound clearly builds in intensity and the characteristic "whop-whop" of the propeller becomes louder and more pronounced, which is the acoustic signature of an object moving towards an observer.\nStep 3: Conclude the direction of movement based on the observed acoustic changes. Since the sound is increasing in volume and the Doppler shift suggests increasing frequency, the plane is getting closer.\n\nTherefore, the plane is getting closer to the observer.\n\nAnswer: \boxed{B}
2025-12-22 19:07:29 | INFO | Q716 id=BV1Sr4y167yH_00-00-01_00-00-05 | pred=A | gold=B | correct=False | running_acc=64.94% | raw=The audio contains the distinct sound of a train horn, which is characterized by a Doppler effect when the source is moving relative to the observer.\n\nStep 1: Analyze the sound. The sound is a train horn.\nStep 2: Listen for changes in pitch (Doppler effect). The pitch of the horn starts relatively high and then drops significantly as the sound passes.\nStep 3: Relate the pitch change to motion. A drop in pitch (lowering frequency) indicates that the sound source is moving away from the observer after passing. If the sound were getting closer, the pitch would be increasing (higher frequency) before the closest point. Since the pitch is clearly dropping, the train has likely already passed the observer or is moving away after being very close.\n\nBased on the characteristic drop in pitch associated with the Doppler effect for a passing train, the train is moving further away.\n\nAnswer: \boxed{A}
